    35	---
    36	
    37	## Definition
    38	
    39	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
    40	open covers, subcovers, finiteness and compactness as in [[def-compact-space]],
    41	and *finite*, *at most countable* and *uncountable* as in [[def-countable]].
    42	
    43	- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **countably compact** when every open cover of $X$ that
    44	  is at most countable has a finite subcover.
    45	- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **Lindel&ouml;f** when every open cover of $X$ has an at
    46	  most countable subcover.
    47	- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ in
    48	  $X$, that is every function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$
    49	  ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]), has a subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ converging to
    50	  a point of $X$, the index map $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ being strictly
    51	  increasing ([[def-sequence]], [[lem-index-map-grows]]).
    52	- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **limit point compact** when every infinite subset
    53	  $A \subseteq X$ has a limit point in $X$, that is a point $p \in X$ every
    54	  neighbourhood $N$ of which satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{p\}) \ne \varnothing$
    55	  ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). Here *infinite* means not finite in
    56	  the sense of [[def-countable]].
    57	- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **$\sigma$-compact** when there is an at most countable
    58	  family $\mathcal{K}$ of compact subsets of $X$ with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{K}$.
    59	- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is **relatively compact in $X$** when its closure
    60	  $\overline{A}$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) is a compact subset of
    61	  $X$.
    62	
    63	A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called countably compact, Lindel&ouml;f,
    64	sequentially compact, limit point compact or $\sigma$-compact when the subspace
    65	$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]), exactly as for
    66	compactness. **Relative compactness is the exception and is deliberately not of
    67	that form**: it is a statement about $A$ *inside* $X$, since $\overline{A}$ is
    68	computed in $X$, and a set may be relatively compact in one space and not in
    69	another that contains it. Every other notion on this list is intrinsic to the
    70	subspace.
    71	
    72	**The countable covers may be listed.** A nonempty at most countable family
    73	$\mathcal{U}$ admits a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$
    74	([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]), so countable compactness says: for
    75	every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of open sets with
    76	$X = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ there are finitely many indices whose sets
    77	already cover $X$. That surjection is produced from the countability assumption
    78	alone and no choice principle is involved; the empty family covers only the empty
    79	space, which is compact anyway.
    80	
    81	**Indexing starts at $0$.** A sequence here is a function on $\mathbb{N}$ and
    82	$\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so a subsequence is
    83	$(x_{n_j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $n_0 < n_1 < \cdots$ and $n_j \ge j$
    84	([[lem-index-map-grows]]). An index range taken from a text that starts at $1$
    85	must be shifted before it is used here.
    86	
    87	**Agreement with the metric definitions.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space carrying
    88	its metric topology $\mathcal{T}_d$ ([[def-metric-topology]],
    89	[[def-metrizable-space]]). Then the three notions that
    90	[[def-metric-compactness-variants]] defines metrically are the three defined
    91	above, read in $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$:
    92	
    93	- *Countably compact.* The open sets used there are the members of
    94	  $\mathcal{T}_d$, so the at most countable open covers are the same families and
    95	  the condition is the same condition, exactly as for compactness itself
    96	  ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]).
    97	- *Sequentially compact.* Convergence of a sequence in the metric sense and in
    98	  the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] agree on a metric topology,
    99	  because the balls around a point are a neighbourhood base at it
   100	  ([[def-metrizable-space]]); the subsequences quantified over are the same.
   101	- *Limit point compact.* A point $p$ is a limit point of $A$ in the metric sense
   102	  when every ball around $p$ meets $A \setminus \{p\}$, and in the sense above
   103	  when every neighbourhood does; the same neighbourhood base makes the two
   104	  conditions one ([[def-metrizable-space]],
   105	  [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
   106	
   107	So no statement below about a metrizable space introduces a second notion, and
   108	every theorem of the metric development about these three properties may be
   109	quoted here once a metric inducing the topology is named. Lindel&ouml;fness,
   110	$\sigma$-compactness and relative compactness have no metric counterpart in this
   111	library and are defined here for the first time.
   112	
   113	## Remarks
   114	
   115	**None of the conditions listed above is compactness by definition.** Countable
   116	compactness restricts the covers tested; Lindel&ouml;fness weakens the conclusion
   117	from finite to at most countable; sequential compactness speaks about sequences
   118	instead of covers; limit point compactness speaks about subsets;
   119	$\sigma$-compactness asks only that the space be assembled from at most countably
   120	many compact pieces; relative compactness is a condition on a subset of an
   121	ambient space. Which implications hold between them, and which need a choice
   122	principle, is [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]]; that some of them fail
   123	to be equivalent is witnessed by the false statements at the end of this page.
   124	
   125	**Why $\sigma$-compactness is not a compactness property at all.** The real line
   126	is $\sigma$-compact, being the union of the compact intervals $[-n, n]$, and it is
   127	not compact; the definition is useful precisely because it names a class of
   128	spaces built out of compact pieces without being compact. The same remark
   129	explains why a $\sigma$-compact space need not be countably compact.
   130	
   131	**Limit point compactness is sometimes called the Bolzano-Weierstrass
   132	property**, and *countably compact* is occasionally used for what is called limit
   133	point compact here. This library uses the four names above with the meanings
   134	given, and writes the condition out whenever the risk of confusion is real.
     1	---
