    30	
    31	## Definition
    32	
    33	The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**
    34	([[def-inductive-set]]),
    35	
    36	$$\mathbb{N} = \omega := \bigcap\,\{\, I : I \text{ is inductive} \,\},$$
    37	
    38	which exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the
    39	Axiom of Infinity supplies one inductive set to intersect within, and Separation
    40	makes the intersection a set). On $\mathbb{N}$ we take
    41	
    42	$$0 := \varnothing, \qquad \sigma(n) := n \cup \{n\},$$
    43	
    44	the distinguished element and the successor function. Thus
    45	$0 = \varnothing$, $1 = \{0\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, $3 = \{0, 1, 2\}$, and in general
    46	$n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is the set of its predecessors.
    47	
    48	## Remarks
    49	
    50	"Smallest" means $\omega \subseteq I$ for every inductive set $I$. This minimality
    51	is exactly the **induction principle** ([[thm-induction-principle]]): a subset of
    52	$\mathbb{N}$ that contains $0$ and is closed under $\sigma$ is itself inductive,
    53	hence contains $\omega = \mathbb{N}$, hence equals $\mathbb{N}$.
    54	
    55	With $0$ and $\sigma$ so defined, $(\mathbb{N}, 0, \sigma)$ satisfies the Peano
    56	axioms ([[def-peano-system]], proved in [[thm-omega-is-peano-system]]), so it is a
    57	**model** of the abstract natural numbers. By categoricity
    58	([[thm-peano-categoricity]]) any other model is uniquely isomorphic to it, so the
    59	particular set-theoretic encoding chosen here is immaterial to every arithmetic
    60	and order property that follows: those are developed from the Peano axioms, not
    61	from the sets themselves.

