    32	---
    33	
    34	## Definition
    35	
    36	Let $X$ be a set and write $\mathcal{P}(X)$ for its power set. A **topology** on
    37	$X$ is a family $\mathcal{T} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ such that
    38	
    39	- **(T1)** $\varnothing \in \mathcal{T}$ and $X \in \mathcal{T}$;
    40	- **(T2)** $\bigcup \mathcal{S} \in \mathcal{T}$ for every $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$;
    41	- **(T3)** $U \cap V \in \mathcal{T}$ for all $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$.
    42	
    43	A **topological space** is a pair $(X, \mathcal{T})$ consisting of a set $X$ and
    44	a topology $\mathcal{T}$ on it; the elements of $X$ are its **points**. When only
    45	one topology is in play we write $X$ for the pair; when several are, the topology
    46	is always named.
    47	
    48	The members of $\mathcal{T}$ are the **open** sets of $(X,\mathcal{T})$. A subset
    49	$F \subseteq X$ is **closed** when its complement $X \setminus F$ is open, and
    50	**clopen** when it is both open and closed. By (T1) both $\varnothing$ and $X$
    51	are clopen. *Open and closed are not opposites*: a set may be neither, and it may
    52	be both, so "not open" is never a synonym for "closed".
    53	
    54	**Finite intersections, and the empty one.** Axiom (T3) is stated for two sets
    55	because that is all that is ever needed: if $n \ge 1$ and
    56	$U_1, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{T}$, then $U_1 \cap \dots \cap U_n \in \mathcal{T}$
    57	follows by iterating (T3) $n-1$ times. The intersection of the **empty** family
    58	of subsets of $X$ is $X$, since the condition "lies in every member of the empty
    59	family" is vacuous; that case is covered by (T1) rather than by (T3), which is
    60	one of the two reasons $X \in \mathcal{T}$ is demanded outright. The other is
    61	that (T2) alone gives only $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$.
    62	
    63	**The closed sets, and the dual axiomatisation.** Put
    64	$\mathcal{C} := \{\, X \setminus U : U \in \mathcal{T} \,\}$, the family of closed
    65	sets. Then
    66	
    67	- **(C1)** $\varnothing \in \mathcal{C}$ and $X \in \mathcal{C}$;
    68	- **(C2)** $\bigcap \mathcal{D} \in \mathcal{C}$ for every **nonempty**
    69	  $\mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$;
    70	- **(C3)** $C \cup D \in \mathcal{C}$ for all $C, D \in \mathcal{C}$.
    71	
    72	Conversely, if a family $\mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ satisfies (C1),
    73	(C2) and (C3), then $\mathcal{T} := \{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{C} \,\}$
    74	is a topology on $X$ whose closed sets are exactly the members of $\mathcal{C}$.
    75	So a topology may be presented equally well by its open sets or by its closed
    76	sets, and this library uses whichever is shorter.
    77	
    78	*Both directions are one computation, and it is carried out here rather than
    79	assumed.* The map $U \mapsto X \setminus U$ is an involution of
    80	$\mathcal{P}(X)$, hence a bijection, and it carries the two De Morgan identities
    81	
    82	$$X \setminus \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i = \bigcap_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \quad (I \ne \varnothing), \qquad X \setminus \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i = \bigcup_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \quad (I \ne \varnothing)$$
    83	
    84	term by term into each other. Given (T1)–(T3): $\varnothing = X \setminus X$ and
    85	$X = X \setminus \varnothing$ lie in $\mathcal{C}$, which is (C1); for nonempty
    86	$\mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathcal{C}$ the family
    87	$\mathcal{S} = \{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{D} \,\}$ lies in $\mathcal{T}$
    88	and $\bigcap \mathcal{D} = X \setminus \bigcup \mathcal{S}$ is closed by (T2),
    89	which is (C2); and $C \cup D = X \setminus ((X \setminus C) \cap (X \setminus D))$
    90	is closed by (T3), which is (C3). Given (C1)–(C3) the same three lines run
    91	backwards, the empty-union case $\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$ being supplied
    92	by (C1) rather than by (C2); and $\{\, X \setminus C : C \in \mathcal{C} \,\}$ has
    93	$\mathcal{C}$ as its family of complements because complementation is an
    94	involution.
    95	
    96	**Comparison of topologies.** If $\mathcal{T}_1$ and $\mathcal{T}_2$ are
    97	topologies on the same set $X$ and $\mathcal{T}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{T}_2$, then
    98	$\mathcal{T}_1$ is **coarser** than $\mathcal{T}_2$ and $\mathcal{T}_2$ is
    99	**finer** than $\mathcal{T}_1$; the comparison is **strict** when the inclusion
   100	is. Inclusion is a partial order on the set of topologies on $X$
   101	([[def-partial-order]]), and it is not a total one: two topologies on the same
   102	set may be incomparable.
   103	
   104	## Remarks
   105	
   106	- **The topology is part of the data.** Two different topologies on one set are
   107	  two different topological spaces, exactly as two different metrics on one set
   108	  are two different metric spaces. Every statement below that says "the space
   109	  $X$" has a topology fixed in advance.
   110	
   111	- **Nothing is assumed beyond (T1)–(T3).** In particular no separation property
   112	  is built into the word *space* here: distinct points need not lie in disjoint
   113	  open sets, singletons need not be closed, and both failures occur in the
   114	  standard topologies defined on the next item. Where a separation property is
   115	  used it is stated as a hypothesis.
   116	
   117	- **This library always writes *coarser* and *finer*.** The synonyms
   118	  *smaller/larger* are unambiguous, but *weaker/stronger* is used in both
   119	  directions in the literature and is avoided here entirely.
   120	
   121	- **Why "arbitrary unions, finite intersections" and not the reverse.** The
   122	  asymmetry is not a convention: it is what makes the metric notion of open set
   123	  ([[def-metric-topology]]) an instance, and an arbitrary intersection of open
   124	  sets genuinely need not be open. The failure is recorded on this page as a
   125	  false statement, with witnesses in two different spaces.

